      instructorVoice:
        "The steelman argument from evolution is perhaps the most culturally powerful: Natural selection acting on random mutations over vast periods of time explains the diversity and complexity of all life on Earth\u2014no designer needed. The evidence from genetics, paleontology, comparative anatomy, and biogeography converges on a common ancestry for all living things. Evolution is not \u2018just a theory\u2019; it is the unifying framework of modern biology.\n\nThis is the argument in its strongest form. How do we respond? First, we distinguish between microevolution (observable changes within kinds\u2014antibiotic resistance, beak variation) and macroevolution (the unobserved transition from single-celled organisms to human beings over billions of years). The former is demonstrable science; the latter is an interpretive framework applied to historical evidence.\n\nGenesis 1:21 states, \u201CAnd God created great whales, and every living creature that moveth, which the waters brought forth abundantly, after their kind, and every winged fowl after his kind: and God saw that it was good.\u201D The phrase \u2018after their kind\u2019 (Hebrew: \u2018min\u2019) appears repeatedly in Genesis 1, indicating that God created distinct categories of life with the capacity for variation within those kinds\u2014but not limitless transformation from one kind to another.\n\nAs Seventh-day Adventists, we hold to a literal, recent, six-day creation as a fundamental belief. This is not anti-science; it is a different interpretive framework applied to the same evidence. The Geoscience Research Institute, an SDA scholarly institution, has published extensively on this. The Sabbath commandment itself anchors our position: \u201CFor in six days the LORD made heaven and earth, the sea, and all that in them is, and rested the seventh day\u201D (Exodus 20:11). If creation was not literal, the Sabbath loses its foundation.",
