        "The steelman case for secular morality goes like this: Humans do not need God to be moral. Empathy, reciprocity, and social cooperation are observable in many species. Moral behavior evolved because groups that cooperated survived better than those that did not. We can ground ethics in human well-being, rational discourse, and social contracts\u2014no divine command needed. In fact, secular societies (Scandinavia, for example) often have lower crime rates than religious ones.\n\nThis is a persuasive argument on the surface. But it confuses moral behavior with moral grounding. The question is not \u2018Can atheists be good people?\u2019\u2014of course they can. The question is \u2018On atheism, is there any objective basis for calling anything truly good or evil?\u2019 If morality evolved for survival, then it is descriptive (this is what humans do) not prescriptive (this is what humans ought to do).\n\nRomans 2:14-15 addresses this directly: \u201CFor when the Gentiles, which have not the law, do by nature the things contained in the law, these, having not the law, are a law unto themselves: Which shew the work of the law written in their hearts, their conscience also bearing witness, and their thoughts the mean while accusing or else excusing one another.\u201D God\u2019s moral law is written on every human heart\u2014which is precisely why atheists can behave morally. But the existence of conscience points to the Lawgiver, not away from Him.\n\nThe SDA position strengthens this further: we affirm the perpetuity of the Ten Commandments as an eternal expression of God\u2019s character. The atheist borrows from this moral capital every time they say something is \u2018truly wrong.\u2019 They can live morally; they cannot ground morality.",
