        "The argument from divine hiddenness is a sophisticated cousin of the problem of evil: If God exists and wants a relationship with humans, why doesn\u2019t He make His existence undeniably obvious? Why doesn\u2019t He appear in the sky and settle the question? The fact that reasonable people can sincerely doubt God\u2019s existence suggests either He doesn\u2019t exist or He doesn\u2019t care about being known.\n\nThis is a serious argument that deserves a serious answer. The SDA Great Controversy framework again provides a uniquely powerful response. God is not hiding\u2014He is revealing Himself progressively while respecting human freedom. If God appeared in overwhelming, undeniable glory, it would produce compliance, not love. Satan\u2019s accusation is that God\u2019s creatures serve Him only out of fear (see Job 1:9-10: \u201CDoth Job fear God for nought? Hast not thou made an hedge about him?\u201D). God must demonstrate that genuine love, freely chosen, is the basis of His government.\n\nIsaiah 45:15 acknowledges this tension: \u201CVerily thou art a God that hidest thyself, O God of Israel, the Saviour.\u201D But this hiding is strategic, not indifferent. Jeremiah 29:13 promises, \u201CAnd ye shall seek me, and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart.\u201D God reveals Himself to those who genuinely seek\u2014He does not force Himself on those who don\u2019t.\n\nMoreover, as SDAs, we believe God has not been silent. He has spoken through Scripture, through the prophetic gift (including Ellen White), through fulfilled prophecy (Daniel 2, 7, 8-9), and through the testimony of creation. The claim that God is hidden ignores the mountain of evidence He has provided for those willing to examine it.",
